needed in many studies of materials, including calculations of infrared (IR) [24], Raman [25,26], and sum frequency generation (SFG) [27] spectra, transport calculations in ionic liquids [28] and superionic crystals [29-31], and simulations of ferroelectric phase transformations [32,33]. These schemes learn from AIMD trajectories, but so far the calculated spectra of liquid water [23] do not match the quality of direct manybody expansions of the dipole moment [34].

Here, we propose an alternative approach based on deep neural networks (DNNs) and maximally localized Wannier functions (MLWFs) [35,36], i.e., electronic orbitals with minimal spatial spread obtained from a unitary transformation of the occupied orbitals in insulators. In spin saturated systems the MLWFs describe electron pairs. Thus, upon assigning charges of \(2 e^{-}\)to the Wannier centers (WCs), i.e., the centers of the MLWF distributions, the electric polarization is the dipole moment \(\boldsymbol{M}\) of the neutral system of point charges made by the WCs and the atomic nuclei. In extended periodic systems, \(\boldsymbol{M}\) includes an arbitrary quantum [37], but its

